Z||000056||Fiero2||11-11-2000||10:46 PM||||I have been watching the news, and following this in the papers. I think it is interesting for the school teachers who are trying to use this election like they always do to teach students how politics work. Or are supposed to anyway. Must be a fun time for those teachers right now. LOL.<P> I have to say that in this post you can easily see who is a supporter of Gore, and who is a supporter of Bush.<BR>The facts so far are that Gore is NOT the one  who originally caused the first Florida recount. It was automatic under Florida law due to the close numbers. Saying that Gore is whining and so forth, is just not true. He has only said that he wants it to be done right, with no rush to judgement. Bush on the other hand is allready setting up his cabinet. And now his people are fighting a recount in court scheduled for Monday. He seems to be pretty scared that a quality hand done recount may show a different winner in some of those areas that had serious irregular problems and uncounted ballots due to bad punching designs. The hand count is also automatic in certain circumstances under Florida law. They have mentioned this over and over in the news, so I don't know why some keep thinking that Gore and his people had anything to do with it. His people did ask for recounts in some districts due to obvious problems, but that was there right under Florida law. That would be Jeb Bush and his states laws by the way :-) Bush and his people have that right also.<P>I don't believe that Jeb Bush had anything to do with the voting problems and missing ballots, other than he is the governor and it is happening on his watch.<P>The ballot punch problem looks like it has effected Illinois now also, so I think that gives even more weight now to those poor voters in Florida who said they had a problem with the poorly designed and misaligned ballot. At first it looked like other states that used a similar "butterfly" ballot like Illinois had had no problems, but now it looks like that is now not the case.<P>Speaking of the confusing ballot design. Many have said that this is the same ballot Florida and other states have used for years. That is incorrect. The lady who designed the "new" ballot has now gotten a lawyer because of the questions arising from her design she made for "this years" ballot. Allthough similar, this is a new ballot design that she says she made with the elderly in mind. That's a good one. Did she try a real world test using test subjects like some elderly people that she says she designed it for in an actual booth to see how it would work? Also, just because she happens to be a democrat doesn't change the fact that it is a pretty poor design. Yes, it was approved by both parties, but the one that was approved is not the final one everyone got stuck with. Oh yeah, the final one was also different in more ways than just having the holes in it.<BR> <BR>The story about the teacher that duplicated the ballot and substituted Disney characters and that her students didn't have any problems voting is a great story. The one problem with that, is that the kids only had to mark the spot with a marker. In the real world in Florida, those who had to use the actual ballot had to deal with the ballot nit fitting right in the punch box.  Obviously the teachers students didn't have that problem.<P>I do agree that the people should have said something right off when they discovered it didn't punch correctly, but I know my grandmother and I bet she wouldn't have said anything either. Would that make her vote not count like those in Palm Beach? That is the big question now I guess.<BR>We shall see. This is history folks. Did you see that||||reg||1
